{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothIndex","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothIndex","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Equiv","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.PDeriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Rat","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Division","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.CommRing","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Determinant.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Int.Units"],"declarations":54,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_det_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_det_ge","kind":"theorem","line":749,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L749-L771","doc":"**The index of a determinant is at least the least permutation-sum of entry indices.**\n\nLeibniz plus the two lemmas above: the determinant is a sum over permutations, so\n`index_sum_ge` takes the least term; each term is a unit times a product, so `index_smul_units`\ndiscards the sign and `index_prod_ge` adds the entries' indices.\n\nThis is what bounds the index of a generalized Wronskian from below in terms of the indices\nof the differentiated entries — the estimate Roth's lemma runs on.  ⚠ It says nothing about\nthe *entries* themselves; relating `index (pderivList D (f j))` back to `index (f j)` is\n`index_le_index_iterate_pderiv_add` above, and that is where the differentiation cost enters. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_C_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_C_mul","kind":"theorem","line":739,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L739-L747","doc":"**Rescaling the polynomial does not move its index.**\n\n`index_smul_units` says this for `ℤˣ`; this says it for any non-zero constant over a domain,\nwhich is what a determinant of derivatives needs — a generalized Wronskian of powers is\nalways a *scalar multiple* of a monomial, and the scalar is an accident of the differentiation\nrather than part of the vanishing behaviour. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.support_C_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.support_C_mul","kind":"theorem","line":733,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L733-L737","doc":"Multiplying by a non-zero constant does not change the support. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_smul_units","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_smul_units","kind":"theorem","line":721,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L721-L731","doc":"Scaling by a unit of `ℤ` — the only scalars the Leibniz expansion produces — leaves the\nindex alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_prod_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_prod_ge","kind":"theorem","line":709,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L709-L719","doc":"**The index of a finite product is at least the sum of the indices.**  The `Finset` form\nof `index_mul_ge`; the empty product is `1`, and `0 ≤ index d α 1` is `index_nonneg`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_sum_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_sum_ge","kind":"theorem","line":696,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L696-L707","doc":"**The index of a finite sum is at least the least index of a summand.**  The `Finset`\nform of `index_add_ge`; the empty sum is `0`, whose index is `⊤`, matching `Finset.inf_empty`\nwith no side condition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_scale_degree_le_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_scale_degree_le_iff","kind":"theorem","line":671,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L671-L687","doc":"**Upper bounds transport across a rescaling:** `index_{c·d} P ≤ θ ↔ index_d P ≤ c·θ`.\n\nThis is the direction the induction *hypothesis* is used in — it supplies upper bounds on the\nfactors' indices, which have to be converted out of the rescaled weights.\n\nThe proof goes through `Finset.inf_le_iff`, which needs the bound to be below `⊤`; a coerced\nrational always is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_scale_degree_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_scale_degree_iff","kind":"theorem","line":657,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L657-L669","doc":"**Lower bounds transport across a rescaling:** `θ ≤ index_{c·d} P ↔ c·θ ≤ index_d P`.\n\nStated as an iff on bounds rather than as `index_{c·d} = index_d / c`, because `WithTop ℚ` has\nno usable division and `⊤` must be handled. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_scale_degree","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_scale_degree","kind":"theorem","line":646,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L646-L655","doc":"**Scaling the degree vector scales the weight inversely.**\n\nNo hypothesis on `c`: at `c = 0` both sides are `0`, since `x / 0 = 0` in `ℚ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_rename_equiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_rename_equiv","kind":"theorem","line":618,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L618-L630","doc":"**Transport along an equivalence.**  A corollary of `index_rename_of_injective`, and the\nlemma that makes reindexing the variable *type* cheap.\n\nThree lines.  That matters for a design decision: an `m`-indexed formulation of Roth's lemma\nneeds `Fin (m+1) ≃ Unit ⊕ Fin m` transport to meet the `σ ⊕ τ` machinery, and this is that\ntransport for the index side.  The height side is free — Mathlib's `coeff_rename_mapDomain`\nalready matches coefficients across a renaming. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_rename_of_injective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_rename_of_injective","kind":"theorem","line":606,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L606-L616","doc":"**The index is invariant under an injective renaming**, with the weights and base point\ntransported. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_mapDomain_of_injective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_mapDomain_of_injective","kind":"theorem","line":594,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L594-L604","doc":"Pushing a multi-index forward along an injection and weighting by `d` is weighting by\n`d ∘ f`: injectivity is what keeps the support sum term-for-term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_rename_of_injective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_rename_of_injective","kind":"theorem","line":581,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L581-L592","doc":"Translating after an injective renaming is renaming after translating by the pulled-back\npoint.  The variables outside the image never see `α`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_of_X_pow_dvd_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_of_X_pow_dvd_translate","kind":"theorem","line":567,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L567-L572","doc":"The index lower bound, phrased as divisibility of the translate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.X_pow_dvd_iff_support","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.X_pow_dvd_iff_support","kind":"theorem","line":558,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L558-L565","doc":"**Divisibility by a power of a variable, as a support condition.**\n\nMathlib has the univariate `Polynomial.X_pow_dvd_iff`; multivariately it has only\n`X_dvd_iff_modMonomial_eq_zero`, the first-power case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.monomial_one_dvd_iff_support","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.monomial_one_dvd_iff_support","kind":"theorem","line":541,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L541-L556","doc":"Divisibility by a monomial is a condition on the support. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_of_forall_le_support","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_of_forall_le_support","kind":"theorem","line":524,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L524-L537","doc":"**High-order vanishing in one variable forces a lower bound on the index.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_idxWeight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_idxWeight","kind":"theorem","line":513,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L513-L522","doc":"A single term is at most the whole weight, since every term is non-negative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_index_iterate_pderiv_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_index_iterate_pderiv_add","kind":"theorem","line":485,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L485-L505","doc":"**The iterated bound:** `k` differentiations in one variable cost at most `k/dⱼ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_index_pderiv_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_index_pderiv_add","kind":"theorem","line":453,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L453-L483","doc":"**The index drops by at most `1/dⱼ` under one differentiation.**\n\nThe `⊤` case is not a technicality to be routed around: when `∂ⱼP = 0` the right-hand side\nis `⊤` and the bound is free, which is exactly the behaviour the `WithTop` return type was\nchosen for at the top of this file. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_le_sub_single_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_le_sub_single_add","kind":"theorem","line":430,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L430-L451","doc":"**The weight drops by at most `1/dᵢ` under one differentiation.**\n\n⚠ **This must be an inequality, and that is what makes it easy.** The `ℕ`-subtraction on\n`σ →₀ ℕ` is truncated, so `s - eᵢ = s` exactly when `sᵢ = 0`, and there the *equality*\n`idxWeight (s - eᵢ) = idxWeight s - 1/dᵢ` is plainly false. Stated as `≤` the degenerate\ncase costs nothing, because `1/dᵢ ≥ 0` makes the bound slack rather than tight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sub_single_of_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sub_single_of_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":422,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L422-L428","doc":"When the variable does not occur, truncated subtraction is the identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_single","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_single","kind":"theorem","line":417,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L417-L420","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.support_pderiv_subset","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.support_pderiv_subset","kind":"theorem","line":400,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L400-L415","doc":"The support of a derivative sits inside the shift of the support by `-eⱼ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_card","kind":"theorem","line":384,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L384-L398","doc":"**The index is at most the number of variables**, when the degree vector dominates `P`.\n\n§9's third property, in the form the arc needs.  It is the first consequence of\n`degreeOf_translate`: the infimum is over the *translated* support, so a degree hypothesis on\n`P` reaches it only because translation preserves degree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_le_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_le_card","kind":"theorem","line":369,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L369-L382","doc":"**Every exponent dominated by the degree vector has weight at most the number of\nvariables** — each term of `idxWeight` is then at most `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_pderiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_pderiv","kind":"theorem","line":339,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L339-L363","doc":"**Translation commutes with partial differentiation:** `(∂ⱼP)(x + α) = ∂ⱼ(P(x + α))`.\n\nThis is the chain rule for a translation, whose Jacobian is the identity. Mathlib has no\nchain rule for `aeval`, so it goes by induction on `P`; the multiplicative step is the only\none with content, and it splits on whether the differentiated variable is the multiplied\none.\n\n⚠ **Why this is the gateway lemma.** `index` is a minimum over the support of the\n*translate*, so any statement about the index of a derivative has to move `pderiv` across\n`translate` before the support is visible at all. Without this, no\nindex-under-differentiation estimate can even be stated in terms of the definition used\nhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_X","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_X","kind":"theorem","line":333,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L333-L337","doc":"Translation sends `xⱼ` to `xⱼ + αⱼ` — the defining property, worth a simp lemma so the\nproofs below never unfold `translate`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_C","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_C","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L328-L331","doc":"Translation is an algebra map, so it fixes constants. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_X_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_X_sq","kind":"theorem","line":305,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L305-L320","doc":"**Validation.**  At the origin with unit weights, `X²` has index exactly `2` — the\norder of vanishing, as it should be.\n\nTogether with `index_X` this shows `index_mul_ge` is **tight** at `X · X`: the bound reads\n`1 + 1 ≤ 2` and both sides are computed, not estimated. A product law that merely held\nloosely would be much weaker evidence that the definition is the intended one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_X","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_X","kind":"theorem","line":297,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L297-L303","doc":"**Validation.**  At the origin with unit weights, `X` has index exactly `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_mul_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_mul_ge","kind":"theorem","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L268-L287","doc":"**The index of a product is at least the sum of the indices.**\n\nThe load-bearing algebraic property, and the reason `index` must return `⊤` at `0` rather\nthan a junk `0`: with a junk value this is *false*, since `index 0 + index Q = index Q`\nwould have to bound `index 0 = 0`.\n\nEvery monomial of `P·Q` is a sum of one from `P` and one from `Q`, and the weight is\nadditive — so the weight of any monomial of the product is a sum of two weights, each\nbounded below by its factor's index. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_add_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_add_ge","kind":"theorem","line":253,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L253-L266","doc":"**The index of a sum is at least the smaller of the two.**\n\nVanishing to order `a` and to order `b` gives vanishing to order `min a b`; cancellation can\nonly *raise* the index, never lower it, which is why this is an inequality. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_iff","kind":"theorem","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L245-L251","doc":"**A lower bound is checked on the support.**  The workhorse for the estimates in Roth's\nlemma: to show the index is at least `c`, show every occurring multi-index has weight at\nleast `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_eq_top_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_eq_top_iff","kind":"theorem","line":230,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L230-L243","doc":"`index = ⊤` exactly for the zero polynomial: an infinite order of vanishing is not\nattainable otherwise. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":223,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L223-L228","doc":"The index is never negative — every multi-index has nonnegative weight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_zero","kind":"theorem","line":219,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L219-L221","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index","kind":"def","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L211-L217","doc":"**The index of `P` at `α`, weighted by `d`.**\n\nThe least weight of a multi-index occurring in `P(x + α)`; `⊤` when `P = 0`, which is the\ncorrect value rather than a convention — the zero polynomial vanishes to infinite order,\nand `Finset.inf` over the empty support delivers it without a side condition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_add","kind":"theorem","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L200-L209","doc":"**The weight is additive on multi-indices.**\n\nThis is the whole reason the index behaves like an order of vanishing: `∑ⱼ iⱼ/dⱼ` is linear\nin `i`, so a monomial split across a product splits its weight too. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_eq_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_eq_sum","kind":"theorem","line":196,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L196-L198","doc":"The weight is a `Finsupp.sum`, which is what makes it additive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_zero","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L193-L194","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L187-L191","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight","kind":"def","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L183-L185","doc":"The **weight** of a multi-index against the degree vector `d`: `∑ⱼ iⱼ / dⱼ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_translate_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_translate_eq","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L175-L181","doc":"**And in fact preserves it exactly** — apply the inequality to `P` and to its\nback-translation.  Free, given `translate_neg_translate`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_translate","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L164-L173","doc":"**Translation does not raise the `j`-degree.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_translate_monomial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_translate_monomial","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L141-L162","doc":"Translation does not raise the `j`-degree of a monomial. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_X_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_X_le","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L126-L139","doc":"`degreeOf j (X i)` is at most the `j`-th exponent of `single i 1`.\n\n⚠ Stated this way rather than with `if i = j then 1 else 0` so that no `[DecidableEq σ]` is\nneeded in the statement, and via `coeff_X'` rather than `degreeOf_X` so that no `[Nontrivial R]`\nis needed either — over the trivial ring `X i = 0` and the bound is merely slack. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L100-L107","doc":"**Translation detects the zero polynomial and nothing else.**\n\n⚠ Deliberately *not* a `simp` lemma: `translate` appears unfolded in several proofs in this\narc, and turning this into a rewrite rule changes what `simp` does to them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_injective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_injective","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L95-L98","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_neg_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_neg_translate","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L80-L93","doc":"Translating back by `-α` undoes `translate α`.\n\n`translate α` is an algebra endomorphism of the polynomial ring; this exhibits its inverse,\nwhich is what makes `index` well behaved at `P = 0` and nowhere else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_mul","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L75-L78","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_add","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L70-L73","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate_zero","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L66-L68","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translate","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean#L62-L64","doc":"Translate a polynomial by a point: `translate α P` is `P(x + α)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}